Hierarchical Clustering
Objective: To perform Hierarchical Clustering using the Agglomerative approach, visualize the dendrogram, and group similar data points into clusters.
Dataset: Iris dataset (using two features: Sepal Length and Sepal Width).

# Hierarchical Clustering using Agglomerative Approach

# Step 1: Import required libraries
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from sklearn.datasets import load_iris
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from sklearn.cluster import AgglomerativeClustering
from scipy.cluster.hierarchy import dendrogram, linkage

# Step 2: Load the Iris dataset
iris = load_iris()
X = iris.data[:, :2]  # Select first two features

print("Dataset shape:", X.shape)

# Step 3: Standardize the data
scaler = StandardScaler()
X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)

# Step 4: Create a dendrogram
linked = linkage(X_scaled, method='ward')

plt.figure(figsize=(12, 6))
dendrogram(linked)
plt.title("Dendrogram - Hierarchical Clustering")
plt.xlabel("Data Points")
plt.ylabel("Euclidean Distance")
plt.show()

# Step 5: Apply Agglomerative Clustering
model = AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=3,linkage='ward')
clusters = model.fit_predict(X_scaled)

# Step 6: Visualize the clusters
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))

plt.scatter(
    X_scaled[:, 0],
    X_scaled[:, 1],
    c=clusters,
    cmap='viridis',
    s=50
)

plt.xlabel("Standardized Sepal Length")
plt.ylabel("Standardized Sepal Width")
plt.title("Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering")
plt.show()

# Step 7: Display cluster assignments
print("\nCluster assignments:")
print(clusters)

print("\nFirst 10 data points and their clusters:")
for i in range(10):
    print("Data Point:", i, "Cluster:", clusters[i])

 

•	Initially, every data point is considered an individual cluster.
•	The two closest data points are merged first.
•	The process continues by merging the closest clusters.
•	Eventually, all data points become part of one cluster.

 
	
Agglomerative Clustering follows a bottom-up approach.
For example, consider five students grouped based on their marks:
•	Initially, each student forms an individual cluster.
•	Students with similar marks are grouped together.
•	The newly formed clusters are progressively merged.
•	Finally, the process produces the required number of clusters.

